"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was significantly elevated at 86. The surgical findings indicate a high-risk profile, including a Gleason 4+3, pathological T4b stage, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and documented lymph node metastasis. The biopsy grade (Gleason 4+4) differs from the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3). The presence of lymph node metastasis is a significant adverse factor.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node metastasis in the context of the overall pathology report, although nodal metastasis was documented. The nodal status was sampled, which reduces uncertainty regarding nodal involvement. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical unknowns that would substantially revise this prognosis. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents a definitive assessment of recurrence risk. The fixed estimated horizon is 30.87 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 24.70–37.05 months; its coverage has not been validated."
